2. Techniques

Two routines can produce the same reveal through different mechanics. One narrows the possible outcomes. Another learns which outcome occurred. A third prepares a response for each outcome. The distinction matters when deciding what to build or practice.

Technique	How it works	Pantheon application
Forcing	Structure a procedure so different actions can converge on a predetermined outcome. In magician’s choice, the performer can interpret a selection as either keeping or removing the selected item.	Route different opening moves toward a shared scene.
Secret peek	Gain access to information after the participant supplies it, then reveal it later. For example, the performer glimpses a word the participant wrote down.	Let a character act on a clue before the player knows the character saw it.
Multiple outs	Prepare several reveals and select the one that fits the outcome.	Write a distinct response for each clue the traveler could bring through the gate.
Cold reading	Start with a plausible inference and refine it as the person responds.	Give the Adaptive Narrator an initial hypothesis it can revise as the player supplies evidence.
Hot reading	Use a specific detail acquired before the encounter and reveal it at a moment when it seems newly discovered.	Bring back an object or phrase established in an earlier scene.
Misdirection	Give attention a clear target while another event happens elsewhere in the scene.	Let a ringing bell draw the eye toward the gate while a figure crosses behind it.
Delayed reveal	Place evidence early and give it significance later. The reveal changes how the audience understands the earlier scene.	Let the traveler see fresh brass in the lock before learning who had reason to replace it.
Branch convergence	Let different actions reach a shared event while carrying forward distinct information, resources, or relationships.	Open the same gate on every route, then let each clue unlock a different next move.

One reveal, three builds

The performer reveals that the traveler chose the bell. A force routes the traveler to the bell. A peek lets the performer learn which object the traveler chose. Multiple outs give the performer a prepared reveal for the bell, key, or paper. The audience can hear the same line in all three versions. The event that makes the line possible changes.

Money Bin entry format

For each technique, record the intended effect, information required, moment the technique acts, and observable result. To test a combination, hold the scene and reveal steady while changing one mechanism. Save the branch map or cue order so the next run can reproduce the change.